 ## Order
 - Cards are listed in window order (row 1 = cards 1-5, row 2 = cards 6-10). Spread categories so the grid has rhythm; neighbours (left/right and above/below) do not share a background colour.
 
-## Variants (3 alternatives of ONE set, not 3 sub-themes)
-- All variants share ONE header: the same set_name, context (the same day), place (the same 2-4 places), palette and motif. Write the header once and copy it into every variant word for word.
-- Variants differ ONLY in which 10 objects are picked from that day. The game designer mixes 30 cards into one final set of 10, so every card must fit into any other variant without looking foreign.
-- Do NOT give variants different routes, professions, accents or sub-themes. Iconic objects may repeat between variants (about a third overlap is fine).
-- Keep every variant faithful to all the rules above; the objects of one variant still must not repeat inside it.
-- One object type (cape, belt, boots, tower...) appears in at most 2 of the 3 variants. Fill the rest with other things of the same day, so the 30 cards give the designer a real choice.
-
 ## No protected IP (stop-list)
 - Generic archetypes are ALLOWED and wanted (cape, domino mask, gloves, round shield, hammer, robot suit, jetpack, starfighter, wizard hat, sword in stone). Forbidden are only SPECIFIC designs and names of known franchises, even renamed: lightsaber, blue milk, cantina, droid, jedi/sith robes, X-wing/TIE silhouettes, macrobinoculars, bat-shaped anything, a spotlight projecting an emblem in the sky, a red-blue phone booth for changing clothes, a red-and-gold Iron Man helmet, a round red-white-blue star shield, Mjolnir's square head with a short leather-wrapped handle, the One Ring, a sorting hat, a golden snitch, horned dragonborn helmets.
 - Test: would a lawyer say "that is THE <franchise> item"? Then change the shape and colours until it is just the archetype.
